Alex Jones was right

Modern wastewater systems were never designed to remove synthetic hormones

Compounds like ethinyl estradiol, the active ingredient in many oral contraceptives, are excreted, enter sewage systems, and can pass through treatment plants. Multiple environmental studies have detected pharmaceutical residues in surface water and, in some regions, treated drinking water.

At certain concentrations, synthetic estrogens are biologically active. In aquatic ecosystems, even very small amounts have been shown to disrupt endocrine function in fish and amphibians.

If these compounds can alter hormone signalling in wildlife at sufficient exposure, what does decades of ultra-low, chronic exposure mean for humans?

Regulators maintain that levels found in drinking water are far below thresholds known to cause measurable effects in people. That’s the current consensus.

But long-term cumulative exposure to mixtures of pharmaceuticals was never part of the original design of municipal water systems.

The real issue isn’t panic.
It’s infrastructure lag.

Our chemistry evolved faster than our filtration.

And asking whether modern treatment systems are equipped to handle hormone-active compounds isn’t conspiracy, it’s a question of engineering, toxicology, and public health.

Water should be the cleanest thing we consume.

If trace endocrine-active compounds are present at all, even at low levels, that’s worth examining seriously, not mocking.
